# Screen control — hold the session across short gaps

When you will use an Android device screen several times in relatively quick succession — especially when a later step depends on the UI end state of an earlier one — **keep `ScreenControlSession` open** (display inversion + presence lease) for the whole multi-step sequence. Do not drop control between steps just because you briefly are not tapping.

## Why

Exiting the session clears inversion and presence. Re-entering re-runs consent, reinverts the glass, and can lose or change the on-screen state you were chaining on. Short idle gaps while you read dumps / decide the next tap are normal — leave the session held.

## Do

- One `with ScreenControlSession(...):` (or on-device equivalent) spanning open → navigate → tap → verify → next taps.
- Keep inversion on during brief gaps (seconds to a few minutes) between related UI actions on the same host. The session keepalive re-asserts inversion and extends the presence lease every ~45s while held.
- Emit **USING** once when you first take the screen; **FREE** only when done with that phone until the next user reply (see HANDOFF).
- At a **batch endpoint** (session `__exit__`), let the session restore the foreground activity that was showing when you started. Do not force `KEYCODE_HOME` at the end of a multi-step script unless you intentionally want the launcher.

## Don't

- Open/close a new session per tap or per probe in the same flow.
- End the session between dependent steps (e.g. “open Settings”, then a new session for “toggle filter”) — that is how Discord (or whatever was foreground) reappears mid-batch.
- Use `STAYTURGID_SKIP_PRESENCE=1` to avoid holding a real session (debug only).
- Leave the user on Aurora/Neo/Settings after a batch finishes when they had Discord (or anything else) open — restore is the default.

## Exception

Independent one-shots with a long idle between them (operator walked away, different host, unrelated task later) may release and re-acquire. When unsure and steps are minutes apart on the same host with a preserved UI, prefer hold.

## Inversion is mandatory for live UI

Display inversion is the visible "agent has the glass" signal. **Always** invert while actively controlling a phone, including when using `STAYTURGID_SKIP_PRESENCE=1` (that flag may skip consent/torch only — never inversion, and input stays gated on inversion).

For overnight / unattended screenshot jobs use `STAYTURGID_PRESENCE_QUIET=1` instead (no torch, vibrate, or dialogs; inversion still on). Prefer that over `SKIP_PRESENCE`.

Do **not** set `STAYTURGID_SKIP_PRESENCE=1` just to avoid a presence timeout on a device a human is watching. Fix presence / hold a normal session instead.

## Cross-project lease (DSCL v1)

Other projects on this Mac may control the same phones (especially **p7a**). Before UI work:

```bash
python3 control/bin/screen_lease.py status
python3 control/bin/screen_lease.py check <host>   # exit 1 = foreign hold
```

`ScreenControlSession` acquires a Mac lease under
`~/.local/state/device-screen-control/leases/` automatically. If another
`holder.project` holds the device, the session **fails closed** — tell the
operator and use **s24** (or wait) instead of FORCE-stealing.

Full protocol + interop prompt for other AIs:
[../docs/modules/screen-control-lease.md](../docs/modules/screen-control-lease.md).
